WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.077
Detection rules depend on telemetry.

00:00:02.077 --> 00:00:06.428
Endpoint logs, authentication events, identity provider records, cloud activity, network data,

00:00:06.428 --> 00:00:08.405
application events, email security data,

00:00:08.405 --> 00:00:11.569
and case management context all provide different perspectives.

00:00:11.569 --> 00:00:17.502
Detection engineers should identify which data sources can actually show the behavior they care about.

00:00:17.502 --> 00:00:20.270
Some activity may require endpoint process data.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:25.412
Other activity may be clearer in identity logs, cloud audit logs, proxy records,

00:00:25.412 --> 00:00:26.598
or application events.

00:00:26.598 --> 00:00:27.785
Visibility limitations matter.

00:00:27.785 --> 00:00:30.948
You cannot reliably detect behaviors you cannot observe.

00:00:30.948 --> 00:00:34.903
If the needed telemetry is missing, delayed, incomplete, inconsistently parsed,

00:00:34.903 --> 00:00:40.045
or not retained long enough, the detection may produce weak or misleading results.

00:00:40.045 --> 00:00:42.813
Telemetry selection also affects cost and usability.

00:00:42.813 --> 00:00:45.582
High-volume data may be expensive or noisy.

00:00:45.582 --> 00:00:49.537
Low-volume data may be easier to review but less complete.

00:00:49.537 --> 00:00:53.492
Good rules account for data quality, field consistency, retention, latency,

00:00:53.492 --> 00:00:56.260
and how analysts will use the signal.
